\title[A min-plus theory of factoring cost exponents]{One Population, One Functional:\\ A Min-Plus Theory of Factoring Cost Exponents\\ and the Newton Polygon of a Benchmark}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{4 September 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a tropical (min-plus) theory of algorithmic cost exponents and apply it to a controlled comparison of five integer-factoring arms measured on a single population of semiprimes with a single cost functional. Writing $k=\log_2 p$ for the bit-size of the hidden prime and measuring work in bits, each arm is an affine profile $k \mapsto c + \alpha k$, i.e.\ a tropical monomial $c \odot k^{\odot \alpha}$; racing arms is tropical addition ($\min$) and composing them is tropical multiplication ($+$). Under this dictionary the qualitative claims of a benchmark become exact statements about the corner locus of a tropical polynomial. We prove: (i) the race of two arms is affine iff their exponents agree, with a quantitative near-parallel bound $|k^\star| \ge \delta/\varepsilon$; (ii) from the curve-budget bound ``the visible set of group orders has size at most $B^2$ inside a group of order about $p$'', reaching success probability $1/2$ costs at least $p/(2B)$ point operations, so with $B=p^\beta$ the elliptic-curve arm traces the tropical line $\alpha = 1-\beta$, and conversely a measured exponent $a$ forces $B \ge p^{1-a}/2$; (iii) Newton-polygon duality for benchmarks: leaders are sorted by decreasing exponent and increasing intercept, an arm strictly above the lower convex hull of $\{(\alpha_i,c_i)\}$ never leads, and an arm on or below the hull leads exactly at the crossing point of its neighbours. Applied to the measured plane (trial division $1.00$/$1.14$, Fermat $0.50$, Pollard rho $0.512$, elliptic curves $0.761$ at $B_1=50$ and $0.718$ at $B_1=250$, with overhead $c_{\ECM}-c_\rho = +3.04$ bits against a $10.29\times$ wall-time ratio), the theory shows the elliptic-curve exponent is strictly interior yet the $B_1=50$ column is a dead arm; that the three exponents are exactly collinear, $0.718 = (43\cdot 0.512 + 206\cdot 0.761)/249$; and hence that the $B_1=250$ column is a hull vertex \emph{iff} its overhead is at most $3.04\cdot 206/249 \approx 2.515$ bits. We also prove a quantisation dichotomy explaining a recorded instrumentation pathology, and that no affine profile represents the true subexponential cost $L_p(1/2,c)$ asymptotically, so every measured interior exponent is a coordinate of the observation window.
\end{abstract}

